## the connector stopped answering the wrong question, and stopped reaching into a drawer that is being taken away

Two changes that are really one, because the second is what made the first
testable.

THE WRONG ANSWER. A bus caller could ask the query log for one client's
lookups and get the WHOLE log back, with ok:true and no way to tell. The
handler appended search, domain and client to the same url.Values key, which
makes a multi-valued key — and AdGuard reads it with url.Values.Get, which
returns only the first. Two of the three filters were accepted and silently
discarded. querylog.Search returns `any`, so the response cannot be filtered
after the fact; the fix has to be at request level. Any one of the three
fields is now accepted and means the same thing, and more than one is refused
naming the offenders. Refusing is strictly better than silently answering a
different question, because nothing downstream can detect the latter.

While in there: the limit is now capped, which the bus manifest has always
promised ("only bounded query responses") and nothing enforced — AdGuard has
no ceiling of its own, it merely defaults to 500 — and the applied limit is
reported back so a truncated answer does not read as the end of the data.
response_status with reason is refused up front instead of forwarding a
combination AdGuard rejects and relaying its internal error.

THE DRAWER. The connector reached into globalContext for statistics, the
query log and the client list. Upstream is dismantling that global a couple of
fields per release — the merge on 2026-08-15 alone brought
AGDNS-4352-rm-global-context-web and AGDNS-4289-rm-global-context-etc-hosts —
so each reach was a future merge conflict on somebody else's schedule.

It now names the three narrow things it actually uses, and ONE function
resolves them. That function takes what it is given and falls back to
globalContext for anything absent, so both worlds work at once: nothing
changes today, and when a field finally leaves the global the compiler stops
in one place instead of three handler bodies. Loudly, which is the point — a
missing dependency should fail the build, not the DNS server.

Two of the three cost nothing to move: stats.Interface and querylog.QueryLog
are ALREADY interfaces upstream, so when those are handed to the connector
directly, the same values satisfy the same shapes and no code here moves.
Only clientsContainer is a concrete struct and needed a described shape.

The handlers also moved off webAPI onto a connector type of their own, so the
fork adds no fields to upstream's structs — the whole surface is constructed
in one place.

AND NOW IT IS TESTED. There were no tests for any of the six functions, nor
for the token check. There are now, including a regression test for the query
log bug that fails against the old behaviour, and one for a subtler bug fixed
here: a stopped AdGuard used to suppress the protection_disabled signal
because a single guard covered both the severity and the signal list — losing
exactly the detail that distinguishes a crash from a deliberate pause.
Severity is now derived from the collected signals rather than defended.
recommended_actions, published in the schema since day one and hard-coded
empty, now says something. | Operator Agent

## swept: Rebuild the Amber Bus mark in client_v2, wired to the connector's real state

The old mark was static React markup in client/, which upstream PR 2715 left
behind when it moved CLIENT_DIR to client_v2/, so it had stopped being built
at all. It also rendered title="Amber Bus connected" unconditionally, which
meant it stayed reassuring during exactly the failure it should have reported.

client_v2 is SolidJS, not React, so this is a rebuild rather than a port, and
it now reads real state: /control/status gains a fork-local `amber_bus` block
(invoke path, exposure mode, whether the token is set), the mark renders
nothing on a server that does not publish it, and says "Amber Bus not
configured" when the token is missing. It sits in the Sidebar as well as the
Header, because the Header is display:none above 1200px.

Also adds amber_bus.client.version.json, which the bus manifest has always
declared as local_manifest_required and which did not exist.

And a .gitignore negation for change.log: upstream's `*.log` rule swallowed
it, so central-git could not record a sweep reason and refused to commit
anything in this repo at all.

Verified: RCP-CLEANUP-BUILD-089232c459c510fb exit [0,0,0]; go test ok; the new
label appears in the built binary, which it did not before the Hub's build
ordering was fixed the same day.

